<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/age-of-revolutions">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1688, almost everyone alive was a <em>subject</em>: born into obedience to a monarch whose
    authority was explained as God-given, owing taxes and service, with political rights only in
    the loose sense that customs limited what a ruler could do. By 1948, the dominant assumption
    — at least on paper — was that legitimate power flows from <em>citizens</em>, equal under law,
    holding rights that governments are built to protect rather than permitted to grant. Between
    those two dates lies the Age of Revolutions: a chain of upheavals in which each one borrowed
    the last one's arguments, tested them in practice, and passed the results onward. The engine
    was a stack of ideas about who rules and why — consent, natural rights, the nation — and each
    revolution stress-tested one. Some succeeded; several devoured their children; all of them
    spread their vocabulary worldwide, which is why "revolution", "citizen", "constitution" and
    "rights of man" are now ordinary political words.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two and a half centuries of overturns — scrub and watch the ideas travel continents" widget="timeline" config={revTimeline} />

  <h2>1688: sovereignty moves — quietly, to Parliament</h2>
  <p>
    The Glorious Revolution looks like a palace intrigue: Parliament invites the Dutch prince
    William of Orange and his wife Mary to take the English throne from Mary's father, James II,
    who flees without a battle. But its settlement was the important part. The Bill of Rights
    (1689) codified that the crown could not raise taxes, keep a standing army, or suspend laws
    without Parliament's consent. Kings henceforth ruled <em>through</em> Parliament —
    constitutional monarchy, established by coup. (Call it near-bloodless only in England: the
    same contest was savage in Ireland, where William's victory at the Boyne in 1690 seeded
    three centuries of conflict.) The deeper precedent was intellectual: a regime could be
    <em>justified by contract</em> rather than by inheritance. That idea had been published in
    1689 by John Locke, whose <em>Two Treatises of Government</em> argued that legitimate
    government rests on the consent of the governed, exists to protect natural rights — life,
    liberty, property — and forfeits its authority when it violates them. Locke's logic was
    radical enough that he published anonymously, from Holland.
  </p>

  <h2>1776: Locke, weaponised</h2>
  <p>
    The American revolutionaries began as conservatives — colonists claiming the traditional
    "rights of Englishmen" against a Parliament in which they had no seats. "No taxation without
    representation" was, initially, a demand to be treated as Britons. Only when reconciliation
    failed did Thomas Jefferson's Declaration of Independence (1776) restate the grievance in
    Locke's language, elevated to "self-evident" truths: all men created equal, endowed with
    rights to life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness, governments deriving "their just powers
    from the consent of the governed". Two things made the outcome world-changing. First, the
    rebels won — the first successful colonial break from a European empire in the modern era,
    proof that the argument could work in practice. Second, the survivors built institutions to
    match: a written constitution (1787) with a deliberate separation of powers (Montesquieu's
    theory, made operational) and a bill of rights added in 1791. A revolution that ends with a
    constitution, rather than a new king, was itself an invention. Its stain, inherited and long
    defended, was slavery — the "all men" of 1776 did not include the enslaved, a contradiction
    that would cost a civil war.
  </p>

  <h2>1789: the nation replaces the crown</h2>
  <p>
    France's crisis was fiscal before it was philosophical: decades of war, including expensive
    support for the American revolution, had left the crown drowning in debt, with servicing it
    consuming roughly half of state revenue. When the Estates-General met in May 1789 for the
    first time in 175 years, the Third Estate — representing everyone who was neither noble nor
    clergy, some 97% of the population — demanded votes by head rather than by order, lost, and
    declared itself a National Assembly: the claim that sovereignty lived in the nation, not the
    crown. In July the Paris crowd stormed the Bastille; in August the Assembly issued the
    <em>Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</em>, the most condensed statement of
    the new order: "Men are born and remain free and equal in rights." Feudal privileges were
    abolished overnight; the Church's lands were confiscated; in 1792 the monarchy itself fell.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then came the lesson every revolution since has had to absorb: how to survive your own
    success. Wartime invasion, civil war in the Vendée and paranoid politics produced the Terror
    (1793–94), in which the Committee of Public Safety executed some 17,000 people by guillotine
    — including the revolution's own leaders, Danton among them. "Revolution devours its
    children" (Vergniaud, on the scaffold) became a pattern, not a quip. Out of the chaos rose
    Napoleon, who in a decade exported the revolution's achievements at bayonet point: the Code
    Napoléon (1804) — equality before the law, careers open to talent, secular administration —
    imposed across Europe, along with the lesson that peoples governed by foreigners could
    imagine themselves nations. Ironically, the man who crowned himself emperor did more than
    anyone to kill hereditary monarchy as a self-evident institution.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the French Revolution was a spontaneous uprising of starving
  peasants. The street crowds of Paris mattered, but the leadership and the programme were
  bourgeois and intellectual — lawyers, doctors and journalists arguing from Rousseau about the
  "general will". Bread prices lit the fire; the fuel was a fully worked theory of sovereignty,
  which is why the revolution produced declarations and constitutions rather than merely a new
  landlord.</p>

  <h2>1804: Haiti, the revolution the world tried to forget</h2>
  <p>
    The French colony of Saint-Domingue was the richest in the Americas — roughly half of
    Europe's sugar and coffee passed through its plantations, worked by about half a million
    enslaved people under brutal conditions. In 1791 they revolted. Under Toussaint
    Louverture and then Jean-Jacques Dessalines, the revolutionaries defeated, in sequence, local
    white militias, Spanish and British forces, and finally the army Napoleon sent in 1802 to
    restore slavery. Haiti declared independence in 1804: the only uprising of enslaved people in
    history to found a nation, and the first Black republic. Its significance was enormous and,
    for a century, deliberately minimised — slaveholding powers refused recognition (the United
    States not until 1862) and France extracted a crushing indemnity for the loss of "property".
    Haiti forced the age's central question: if rights are universal, are they universal for
    everyone? The silence that followed the question is part of the history.
  </p>

  <h2>1848: the dress rehearsal</h2>
  <p>
    In one spring, revolutions broke out across around fifty European states — France (where the
    monarchy fell again, for a republic), the German and Italian states, the Habsburg Empire,
    Poland. The demands had converged: constitutions, elected parliaments, freedom of the press,
    and — the new element — national self-rule for peoples who shared a language and history. Almost
    every rising was defeated within a year or two; the alliance of thrones held. Yet 1848 set the
    agenda for the next seventy years. Within a generation, Italy (1861, completed 1870) and
    Germany (1871) had unified as nation-states, France was a republic again, and serfdom and
    absolutism were permanently on the defensive. The Springtime of Peoples failed and, in
    failing, taught its ideas to the organisers of everything that followed.
  </p>

  <h2>Revolutions beyond Europe: Japan's borrowed modernity</h2>
  <p>
    Not every transformation needed to overthrow a king. When American warships forced Japan's
    ports open in 1853–54, the shogunate's inability to respond discredited it; in 1868 reformers
    "restored" the emperor as a symbol while dismantling feudal rule. The Meiji government then
    executed one of history's fastest modernisations on purpose: state-built railways and mines,
    universal conscription (1873), compulsory primary education, and missions abroad (the
    Iwakura mission of 1871–73, with dozens of senior officials) to copy constitutions, industry
    and universities from the most advanced available models. Within a generation Japan defeated
    China (1895) and Russia (1905) — the first non-European state to industrialise and to beat a
    European great power, shattering the assumption that the revolutionary-industrial package was
    a Western monopoly.
  </p>

  <h2>1917: revolution against the idea of revolution's goals</h2>
  <p>
    Russia's February Revolution (1917) belonged to this story's mainstream: war-weariness and
    autocracy brought down three centuries of Romanovs, and moderates installed a republic. The
    October seizure of power by the Bolsheviks then bent the tradition in a new direction. Karl
    Marx had recast "rights of man" as a shield for property and proposed that history itself
    moved through class struggle toward a workers' state. The Bolsheviks put that claim in
    power — one-party rule in the name of the people, expropriation in the name of equality — and
    the split between liberal-revolutionary and socialist-revolutionary traditions organised
    world politics for the rest of the twentieth century. The lesson repeated: seizing power
    proved easier than building the institutions the dreamers had promised, and the victims were
    counted in millions.
  </p>

  <h2>1947–48: empires end, rights go universal</h2>
  <p>
    Two world wars bankrupted the European empires and discredited the racial hierarchies they
    had rationalised. Indian independence in 1947 — after decades of constitutional campaigning
    and mass non-cooperation, a revolution conducted largely without arms — ended two centuries of
    British rule and opened the wave of decolonisation that would create over eighty new states.
    And in December 1948 the new United Nations adopted the Universal Declaration of Human
    Rights: thirty articles asserting rights that governments "shall not" revoke because, in the
    Declaration's premise, they never granted them. It is the age of revolutions' closing
    argument — the citizen's claim, extended to every person on earth, written by a committee
    chaired by someone (Eleanor Roosevelt) whose own country still disenfranchised millions.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Why revolutions happen where they do.</strong> Historians argue about weightings.
    Structural accounts point to fiscal crisis (France's half-of-revenue debt service), state
    building that created educated, excluded middle classes, and demonstrator effects (the
    American Revolution made French debt worse <em>and</em> French ideas sharper — veterans of
    Yorktown came home fluent in republicanism). Contingency accounts point to choices: Louis
    XVI's indecision, James II's miscalculations, the Bolsheviks' audacity. The mature view is
    Theda Skocpol's blunt summary — "states make revolutions" — old regimes fall when fiscal-military
    strain meets a organised alternative elite; ideology decides what is built on the rubble.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The word itself has a history.</strong> "Revolution" entered politics from astronomy —
    a <em>revolution</em> was a full circuit, a return to a starting point (as in Copernicus's
    <em>De revolutionibus</em>). The Glorious Revolution was welcomed as a restoration of ancient
    liberties, a wheel coming round. Only after 1789 did the word acquire its modern sense:
    irreversible transformation, a break with the past rather than a return to it. Political
    vocabulary was one of the revolutionaries' most durable products.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The franchise took two centuries and is not finished.</strong> Each revolution
    widened "the people" and then stopped at its own comfort line: the French Assembly of 1789
    gave the vote to property-paying men only; the American republic enfranchised white men
    largely, women not until 1920 in the US, and Black Americans' voting rights were enforced
    only a century after that. New Zealand extended the vote to women in 1893 — first among
    self-governing states. The pattern — circles widening under pressure, each widening
    contested as unthinkable in its day — is the best single index of how the subject-becoming-citizen
    project was actually going.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Assessing the ledger.</strong> A serious balance sheet must hold both columns: the
    Terror, Napoleon's wars (estimates run to 3–3.5 million military dead), the Soviet famines —
    and the abolition of feudal privilege, the spread of written constitutions (now essentially
    universal), the codification of rights, and the end of legalised empire. Hobsbawm's verdict
    is a useful frame: the "dual revolution" — French political and British industrial — created
    the modern world's twin templates, and every later argument about how to live together is
    conducted in their vocabulary. The revolutions' greatest product was not any single state but
    the expectation that the political order is <em>made</em> by people, and can therefore be
    remade — for better and, repeatedly, for worse.
  </p>
